speechtext:
A committee* of the
citizens of Winnipeg sent representatives to this city. They met the government and presented their case. They presented it also in writing. The attitude that may be taken with respect to the matters referred to will depend altogether on the general position as indicated by the province of Manitoba and also the municipalities as they relate their position to that of the province. Whether or not the works mentioned will be undertaken has not been, decided, and naturally will not be until such time as this measure having been adopted the activities under it will begin; but if every member of the house were to rise in his place and mention the works that might be done we would remain here rather a long time. For the information of the house I may say that I have asked the Minister of Labour, as soon as this bill becomes law, to communicate with every mem-22110-284}
her of this house, regardless of political affiliations, and ask him for just such information as that to which the hon. gentleman has directed attention, and the Minister of Labour is making the necessary effort in that direction. It was thought very undesirable to send out and ask for information of that kind before this bill was submitted to the house; for it would have involved such claims as might have made it almost impossible for the government to contemplate the position with any degree of equanimity.
